["In formal writing: “Is it ___ you want to see?”",["who","whom","whose","who's"],1,"You want to see him, so the object form: “whom.”",["Common in speech, but formal writing uses “whom” here.","","“Whose” shows ownership.","“Who's” means “who is.”"],"P"],
["“A number of employees ___ asked for remote work.”",["has","have","is","was"],1,"“A number of” means several, so it's plural.",["“The number” would be singular. “A number of” is plural.","","Singular, and the wrong verb.","Singular, and the wrong verb."],"A"],
["“Physics, along with chemistry, ___ required.”",["are","is","were","have been"],1,"The subject is “physics,” which is singular. “Along with chemistry” is extra.",["“Along with” doesn't make the subject plural.","","Plural.","Plural."],"A"],
["“None of the cake ___ left.”",["were","was","are","have been"],1,"With an amount you can't count, like cake, “none” is singular.",["Plural.","","Plural, and present tense.","Plural."],"A"],
["“Yesterday I ___ the book on the table.”",["lay","laid","lain","layed"],1,"“Laid” is the past tense of “lay,” to put something down.",["“Lay” is the past tense of “lie,” to recline, which has no object.","","“Lain” is the past participle of “lie.”","Not a word."],"V"],
["“The documents have ___ there all week.”",["laid","lain","layed","lay"],1,"Documents recline with no object, so “lie.” Its past participle is “lain.”",["“Laid” needs an object: someone laid them there.","","Not a word.","After “have,” you need the participle."],"V"],
["Which has a dangling modifier?",["After reading the report, I had questions.","After reading the report, questions came up.","I had questions after reading the report.","Questions came up after I read the report."],1,"Questions didn't read the report.",["“I” read the report. Correct.","","Clear about who read it.","Clear about who read it."],"V"],
["Rewrite in active voice: “The ball was thrown by Jo.”",["Jo threw the ball.","The ball threw Jo.","Jo was thrown the ball.","Thrown was the ball by Jo."],0,"Put the doer first.",["","Reverses the meaning.","Still passive, and changes the meaning.","Inverted and still passive."],"V"],
["“If it ___ tomorrow, we'll cancel.”",["rains","rained","would rain","had rained"],0,"A real possibility in the future uses the present tense after “if.”",["","Past tense signals an unreal situation.","Don't use “would” in the “if” part.","That's for an unreal past."],"S"],
["Which sentence about the vote is in passive voice?",["The board will vote.","The vote was delayed.","We delayed the vote.","They are voting."],1,"The receiver comes first, and the doer is missing.",["Active.","","Active.","Active."],"V"],
["“I fixed the printer ___.”",["myself","me","I","mine"],0,"“Myself” adds emphasis: I did it, not someone else.",["","You'd never say “I fixed it me.”","“I” can't come at the end here.","“Mine” shows ownership."],"P"],
["In formal writing: “Either of the options ___ fine.”",["are","is","were","have been"],1,"On its own, “either” is singular.",["Common in speech, but formal writing treats “either” as singular.","","Plural.","Plural."],"A"],
["Which keeps the tense consistent?",["She opened the file and sees the error.","She opened the file and saw the error.","She opens the file and saw the error.","She had open the file and sees the error."],1,"Both verbs are in the past tense.",["Jumps from past to present.","","Jumps from present to past.","“Had open” is wrong, and the tense jumps."],"V"],
["In formal writing: “It's time we ___.”",["leave","left","leaving","will leave"],1,"After “it's time,” formal writing uses the past form for something that should already be happening.",["Common in speech, but the formal form is “left.”","","Ungrammatical.","Doesn't fit after “it's time.”"],"S"],
["“Neither he nor ___ was invited.”",["I","me","myself","mine"],0,"It's part of the subject, so the subject form “I.”",["","You'd never say “me was invited.”","“Myself” can't be the subject.","“Mine” shows ownership."],"P"]
