["“Smog” (smoke + fog) is a:",["Blend","Clipping","Acronym","Compound"],0,"Parts of two words merged into one.",["","Clipping shortens one word.","An acronym uses first letters.","A compound joins two whole words."],"W"],
["“Gym” from “gymnasium” is a:",["Clipping","Blend","Back-formation","Borrowing"],0,"A longer word cut short.",["","A blend merges two words.","Back-formation removes what looks like a suffix to make a new word type.","It came from English itself."],"W"],
["“NASA,” said as one word, is a(n):",["Acronym","Initialism","Clipping","Blend"],0,"Built from first letters and said as a word.",["","An initialism is said letter by letter, like FBI.","Clipping shortens one word.","A blend merges two words."],"W"],
["“FBI,” said letter by letter, is a(n):",["Acronym","Initialism","Blend","Clipping"],1,"Built from first letters and said letter by letter.",["An acronym is said as a word, like NASA.","","A blend merges two words.","Clipping shortens one word."],"W"],
["The verb “burgle” came from the older noun “burglar.” This is:",["Back-formation","Clipping","Blending","Conversion"],0,"People removed what looked like a suffix to make a new word.",["","Clipping shortens a word without changing its type.","Blending merges two words.","Conversion changes a word's type without changing its form."],"W"],
["“Text” used as a verb, as in “text me,” is:",["Conversion","Clipping","Blending","Borrowing"],0,"The word changes jobs, from noun to verb, without changing form.",["","Nothing was shortened.","Only one word is involved.","It's an English word."],"W"],
["“Kindergarten” in English is a:",["Borrowing","Blend","Clipping","Acronym"],0,"English took it from German.",["","It wasn't formed by merging English words.","It wasn't shortened.","It isn't built from first letters."],"W"],
["“Sunflower” was formed by:",["Compounding","Blending","Clipping","Back-formation"],0,"Two whole words joined into one.",["","In a blend, parts of words merge. Here both words are whole.","Nothing was shortened.","Nothing was removed."],"W"],
["A word invented from scratch as a brand name, like “Kodak,” is a:",["Coinage","Clipping","Blend","Borrowing"],0,"A brand-new word with no source words.",["","It wasn't shortened from anything.","It doesn't merge existing words.","It wasn't taken from another language."],"W"],
["The study of word structure is:",["Morphology","Syntax","Phonetics","Pragmatics"],0,"Morphology studies how words are built from morphemes.",["","Syntax studies sentence structure.","Phonetics studies speech sounds.","Pragmatics studies meaning in context."],"F"],
["The study of which sounds make a difference in meaning in a language is:",["Phonology","Phonetics","Semantics","Syntax"],0,"Phonology studies sound patterns and contrasts.",["","Phonetics studies how sounds are physically made.","Semantics studies meaning.","Syntax studies sentence structure."],"F"],
["Sociolinguistics studies:",["Language in society, like dialects and social groups","How speech sounds are made","Sentence structure","Only word origins"],0,"It looks at how language varies by region, class, and group.",["","That's phonetics.","That's syntax.","That's etymology."],"F"],
["Etymology is the study of:",["Word origins and history","Insects","Sentence structure","Speech sounds"],0,"Etymology traces where words come from.",["","That's entomology, a common mix-up.","That's syntax.","That's phonetics."],"F"],
["Psycholinguistics studies:",["How the mind produces and understands language","Ancient texts","Spelling rules","Regional dialects"],0,"It looks at language in the brain: learning, reading, and speaking.",["","That's philology.","That's orthography.","That's dialectology, part of sociolinguistics."],"F"],
["Which word has a suffix that changes its word class?",["cats","walked","happiness","taller"],2,"-ness turns the adjective “happy” into a noun.",["The plural -s keeps it a noun.","The past tense -ed keeps it a verb.","","The comparative -er keeps it an adjective."],"M"],
["Which ending is inflectional (it changes the form, not the word)?",["-ness","-ment","-ed","-ful"],2,"-ed marks past tense on the same verb.",["-ness creates a new noun.","-ment creates a new noun.","","-ful creates a new adjective."],"M"],
["“Brunch,” “motel,” and “smog” are all:",["Blends","Clippings","Acronyms","Borrowings"],0,"Each merges parts of two words.",["","Clippings shorten one word.","Acronyms use first letters.","All three were made in English."],"W"],
["A dialect is:",["A variety of a language tied to a region or group","An error in grammar","A dead language","A writing system"],0,"Dialects have their own consistent rules.",["","Dialects aren't errors. They follow their own rules.","Dialects are living varieties.","A writing system is a script."],"F"]
